e  pools of young people congregating  together often on or near college  university campuses and then you had  those terrible realities of the terrible  realities of racism and war and I think  that enabled a large number of young  people to be receptive to different  possibilities and then you had these key  figures like Ginsberg and Kerouac and  Leary and Kesey but you also had the the  great bands of the era you know and the  great musicians of a more solitary cast  you know from Bob Dylan to the Beatles  to the San Francisco sound groups  airplane Quicksilver Grateful Dead and  so forth and I think that young people  were were drawn to messages that were  being imparted messages of a challenging  of a probing of a provocative cast and  sometimes of a of a radical nature  contesting the verities that were in  place contesting American institutions  and even contesting the way people  interacted with one another the way  people fought and the way they behaved  there's a very radical  facet of the counterculture that I that  I highlight there were revolutionary  possibilities  I think inherent in some of this which  is why it's perceived to be as dangerous  as it was considered by some of the  powers that were and it was treated  accordingly there are other avenues  there's the setting up of an underground  press syndicate so underground  newspapers cropped up there's new kind  of comics that are in in place during  the spear we think of Robert Crumb and  and Gilbert Shelton and others the rag  is the underground newspaper in in  Austin in the 1960s and I and I talk  about it again in that chapter on that  Texas capital city and it was and this  incredible compendium that publication  was I mean it you can go back and look  at the issues and you can get these  snapshot looks of so much that was going  on these w the counterculture but the  other movements of the of the entire  period really interesting matter to  reflect on and I grappled with this as  best I can 